%I A083818 #36 Oct 07 2026 16:42:52
%S A083818 1,37,397,3997,39997,399997,3999997,39999997,399999997,3999999997,
%T A083818 39999999997,399999999997,3999999999997,39999999999997,
%U A083818 399999999999997,3999999999999997,39999999999999997,399999999999999997,3999999999999999997,39999999999999999997,399999999999999999997
%N A083818 Numbers k such that 2k-1 is the digit reversal of k.
%C A083818 a(n) = 1 + 36 + 360 + 3600 + 36000 + ..., for a total of n terms.
%C A083818 a(n) = 1 + sum of first n-1 terms of the geometric progression with first term 36 and common ratio 10.
%C A083818 More generally, in base b the equation rev(k) = 2k-1 has a solution with n >= 2 digits if and only if b == 1 (mod 3). For b = 3m+1 the n-digit solution is unique: k = (m+1)*b^(n-1) - m, with base-b digits (m, b-1, ..., b-1, 2m+1). This sequence is the case b = 10, m = 3. The proof follows Radcliffe's; his lemma that no number is exactly doubled by reversal (rev(k) = 2k only for k = 0) holds for b == 1 (mod 3) but fails in other bases, e.g. 13_5 = 8 and 31_5 = 16 in base 5. - _Vincent Weber_, Sep 20 2026
%H A083818 Paolo Xausa, <a href="/A083818/b083818.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..1000</a>
%H A083818 <a href="/index/Rec#order_02">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (11,-10).
%F A083818 a(n) = 1 + 36*A000042(n-1).
%F A083818 a(n) = 4*10^(n-1) - 3.
%F A083818 From _Elmo R. Oliveira_, Jun 12 2025: (Start)
%F A083818 G.f.: x*(26*x+1)/((x-1)*(10*x-1)).
%F A083818 E.g.f.: (13 - 15*exp(x) + 2*exp(10*x))/5.
%F A083818 a(n) = 11*a(n-1) - 10*a(n-2) for n >= 3. (End)
%e A083818 2*37 - 1 = 73.
%t A083818 4*10^Range[0, 25] - 3 (* _Paolo Xausa_, Oct 07 2026 *)
%o A083818 (PARI) my(x='x+O('x^22)); Vec(x*(1+26*x)/((1-x)*(1-10*x))) \\ _Elmo R. Oliveira_, Jun 12 2025
%Y A083818 Cf. A000042, A083811, A083812, A083813.
%Y A083818 Digit reversals of A169830.
%Y A083818 Cf. A083420 (the base-4 case, b = 4, m = 1).
